1
00:00:00,032 --> 00:00:08,918
[MUSIC]

2
00:00:08,918 --> 00:00:12,360
Welcome to the module on
Android Concurrency Frameworks.

3
00:00:12,360 --> 00:00:15,280
Although the standard Java threading,
synchronizer, and

4
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:18,020
coordination features,
covered in the previous module,

5
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:21,500
provide the foundation for
Android's concurrency mechanisms.

6
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:24,320
They can be tedious and
error-prone to use, and

7
00:00:24,320 --> 00:00:28,490
don't shield application developers
from Android's design constraints,

8
00:00:28,490 --> 00:00:31,290
such as its non-thread safe GUI libraries,
and

9
00:00:31,290 --> 00:00:35,289
its limits on the amount of time the one
the only user interface thread can block.

10
00:00:36,590 --> 00:00:40,840
Therefore, to ensure the concurrency
can be used correctly, efficiently and

11
00:00:40,840 --> 00:00:45,530
scalably in applications and middleware,
as well as to elevate the complexity of

12
00:00:45,530 --> 00:00:50,610
its design constraints, Android provides
several concurrency frameworks that allow

13
00:00:50,610 --> 00:00:54,270
background threads to run in
parallel on multiple cause.

14
00:00:54,270 --> 00:00:56,940
And communicate in various ways
with its user interface thread.

15
00:00:58,180 --> 00:01:01,720
This module focuses on two
Android concurrency frameworks.

16
00:01:01,720 --> 00:01:05,280
First we cover its handlers,
runables and messages.

17
00:01:05,280 --> 00:01:09,360
Or hammer framework, which provides
a loosely connected set of classes,

18
00:01:09,360 --> 00:01:13,710
that allow long duration operations to run
in one ore more background threads, and

19
00:01:13,710 --> 00:01:17,550
then publish the results of these
operations to the user interface thread.

20
00:01:17,550 --> 00:01:20,990
Second, we cover Androids
async task framework,

21
00:01:20,990 --> 00:01:23,480
which provides a more tightly
connected set of classes,

22
00:01:23,480 --> 00:01:27,020
that executes long duration operations
in one or more background threads.

23
00:01:27,020 --> 00:01:30,450
And publish the results to the user
interface thread without manipulating

24
00:01:30,450 --> 00:01:33,790
threads, handlers, messages or
runables explicitly.

25
00:01:35,120 --> 00:01:37,970
We'll explore the structure and
functionality of these frameworks, and

26
00:01:37,970 --> 00:01:40,950
show how to apply them to
develop concurrent programs.

27
00:01:40,950 --> 00:01:43,960
Such as a threaded downloads
application that implements three

28
00:01:43,960 --> 00:01:48,470
concurrency models using the Android
Hammer, and AsyncTask frameworks.

29
00:01:48,470 --> 00:01:51,430
We also demonstrate how Android
concurrency frameworks are applied to

30
00:01:51,430 --> 00:01:54,110
its middleware and packaged applications.

31
00:01:54,110 --> 00:01:56,940
As well has how its frameworks
are enhanced, by the application of

32
00:01:56,940 --> 00:02:01,790
Gang of Four patterns, such as template
method, strategy, and factory method.

33
00:02:01,790 --> 00:02:07,007
And POSA patterns, such as active object,
command processor, half sync half async,

34
00:02:07,007 --> 00:02:08,777
and threat specific storch.

35
00:02:08,777 --> 00:02:18,409
[MUSIC]

